from pathlib import Path
import json
base=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2];root=base/'client/src/dev/results/torus-firefox-stress-2026-10-28'
data=json.loads((root/'summary.json').read_text());assets='../src/dev/results/torus-firefox-stress-2026-10-28/'
labels={'nature':'Nature','copper-metropolis':'Copper Metropolis','aether-archipelago':'Aether Archipelago'}
names={'control':'Ordinary view','dense':'Dense wide view','turn':'Rapid turning','move':'Translation'}
rows=[];costs=[];pictures=[];links=[];moving=[]
for w in data['worlds']:
 terrain=w['terrain'];label=labels[terrain]
 m=w['modes']['move'];moving.append(f"<tr><td>{label}</td><td>{m['native']['fps']:.1f}</td><td>{m['firefox']['fps']:.1f}</td><td>{m['firefox']['cpu']['mean']:.2f}</td><td>{m['firefox']['gpu']['mean']:.2f}</td></tr>")
 for mode,m in w['modes'].items():
  n=m['native'];f=m['firefox'];runs=lambda s:' / '.join(f'{v:.1f}'for v in s['blockFps'])
  rows.append(f'<tr><td>{label}<br>{names[mode]}</td><td>{n["fps"]:.1f}<small>{runs(n)}</small></td><td>{f["fps"]:.1f}<small>{runs(f)}</small></td><td>{n["p95"]:.2f} / {f["p95"]:.2f}</td><td>{n["onePercentLow"]:.1f} / {f["onePercentLow"]:.1f}</td><td>{n["over33msPercent"]:.2f}% / {f["over33msPercent"]:.2f}%</td></tr>')
  costs.append(f'<tr><td>{label}<br>{names[mode]}</td><td>{f["cpu"]["mean"]:.2f}</td><td>{f["renderCpu"]["mean"]:.2f}</td><td>{f["terrainCpu"]["mean"]:.3f}</td><td>{f["gpu"]["mean"]:.2f}</td><td>{f["visibleFaces"]["mean"]:,.0f}</td><td>{f["nearNotReadyPercent"]:.1f}% / {f["farPendingPercent"]:.1f}%</td></tr>')
  index={'control':7,'dense':6,'turn':5,'move':4}[mode]
  images=''.join(f'<figure><a href="{assets}{browser}/{terrain}/stress-{index}-{mode}.png"><img width="2560" height="1440" loading="lazy" src="{assets}{browser}/{terrain}/stress-{index}-{mode}.png" alt="{label}, {names[mode]}, {caption}"></a><figcaption>{caption}</figcaption></figure>'for browser,caption in [('minecraft','Minecraft / Sodium + DH torus mod'),('firefox','Space / native Firefox')])
  pictures.append(f'<section class="pair" data-world="{terrain}" data-case="{mode}" {"" if terrain=="nature" and mode=="dense" else "hidden"}><div class="pictures">{images}</div></section>')
 links.append(f'<li>{label}: <a href="{assets}minecraft/{terrain}/torus-stress-minecraft.json.gz">native raw frames</a>; <a href="{assets}firefox/{terrain}/result.json.gz">Firefox raw frames and GPU timestamps</a></li>')
html=f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Firefox and Minecraft: matched torus stress test</title>
<style>body{{margin:0;background:#101722;color:#e7edf5;font:16px/1.65 system-ui}}main{{max-width:1400px;margin:auto;padding:44px 28px}}h1{{font-size:42px;line-height:1.15;max-width:950px}}h2{{margin-top:44px}}p{{max-width:1150px;color:#bdcddd}}.tag{{color:#7fdaca;letter-spacing:2px;font-size:12px}}.note{{padding:18px;border-left:3px solid #7fdaca;background:#1c2b3f}}a{{color:#8bc7ff}}table{{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font:14px/1.5 system-ui;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}}th,td{{padding:12px;border-bottom:1px solid #344154;text-align:right}}th:first-child,td:first-child{{text-align:left}}th{{color:#9cddd3}}small{{display:block;font-size:11px;color:#9cacc1}}.scroll{{overflow:auto}}.pictures{{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:16px}}figure{{margin:0}}img{{width:100%;height:auto;display:block}}figcaption{{font-size:13px;color:#adbed0}}select{{font:inherit;padding:8px;background:#1c2b3f;color:white;border:1px solid #526881}}.pair[hidden]{{display:none}}@media(max-width:800px){{.pictures{{grid-template-columns:1fr}}h1{{font-size:32px}}}}</style>
<main><div class="tag">MATCHED APPLICATION STRESS TEST · 28 OCTOBER 2026</div><h1>Firefox and Minecraft,<br>under the same terrain stress.</h1><p><a href="#findings">Key findings</a> · <a href="#performance">Frame performance</a> · <a href="#costs">Firefox costs</a> · <a href="#images">Image comparison</a></p>
<p class="note"><strong>Common load:</strong> 2560 × 1440 actual rendered pixels, no MSAA, one-metre detail throughout the same eight original terrain zones, 8-chunk near radius and 576-chunk far radius. Fog is off. DH requires 512 fully uploaded detail-0 sections with PIXEL_ART vertical quality; Space requires all eight finest sources and a fixed 1 m mip. This is a bounded 6.25% world fixture, not a full-world maximum-load claim.</p>
<p>Ordinary view uses 70° vertical FOV and 18° downward pitch. Dense view uses 100° and 55° to put more terrain on screen. Turning sweeps ±90° over a 6 s cycle. Translation follows a 64 m × 32 m oscillating route over the same period. The actual projection matrix is checked. Each case runs twice in forward/reverse order, with 6 s warmup and 12 s measurement per block, after 60 s steady warmup. Native and Firefox run sequentially.</p>
<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> these are whole-application frame intervals. Native FPS is uncapped; Firefox presentation is refresh-limited. Lighting, reconstruction, native gameplay overhead and browser overhead remain different. A ratio here is not an isolated rendering-algorithm speedup. Moving frames include near-terrain streaming; initial world preparation and screenshot saving are excluded, but long timed frames are retained.</p>
<h2 id="findings">What the stress cases expose</h2><p>The strongest practical separation appears during translation. The table below reports whole-application results; browser limits and remaining image differences prevent interpreting the ratio as a pure algorithm speedup. Compare dense/turn/move CPU and GPU spans below before attributing the loss to triangle processing.</p><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Moving terrain</th><th>Native FPS</th><th>Firefox FPS</th><th>Firefox CPU ms</th><th>Firefox GPU span ms</th></tr></thead><tbody>{''.join(moving)}</tbody></table></div>
<p class="note"><strong>Quality finding:</strong> Space's wide Nature capture shows visible gaps along the near/far boundary even after settling; the native counterpart does not show those gaps. One-metre source cells therefore do not establish equal final image quality. These are current-renderer stress results, not a strict equal-quality victory for either algorithm. The screenshots preserve the defect instead of concealing it.</p>
<p>Prioritize near/far coverage correctness, then measure moving terrain preparation, publication and command submission separately. The coverage shader currently quantizes chunk coordinates before interpolation; this is a concrete candidate for investigating the boundary gaps, not a confirmed diagnosis from timing data. No production renderer defaults were changed by this stress follow-up.</p>
<h2 id="performance">Average speed and slow frames</h2><p>FPS cells show the pooled result, with the two block results below. Paired columns are Minecraft / Firefox. P95 is the 95th-percentile frame interval. “1% low” is 1000 divided by the mean of the slowest 1% frame intervals. The two repetitions describe variability, not statistical confidence intervals.</p><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Terrain / case</th><th>Native FPS</th><th>Firefox FPS</th><th>P95 ms<br>Native / Firefox</th><th>1% low FPS<br>Native / Firefox</th><th>Frames &gt; 33.3 ms<br>Native / Firefox</th></tr></thead><tbody>{''.join(rows)}</tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="costs">Firefox: where the frame goes</h2><p>Render and distant-update CPU spans are parts of the full CPU callback, not additional costs to add. GPU spans are asynchronous WebGPU pass envelopes; native GPU time is not instrumented. CPU and GPU overlap. Firefox's timer precision limits small subspan interpretation. Near-not-ready and far-pending percentages report the frames observing those states, not missing-pixel percentages.</p><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Terrain / case</th><th>CPU ms</th><th>Render CPU ms</th><th>Distant update ms</th><th>GPU span ms</th><th>Submitted far faces</th><th>Near not ready / far pending</th></tr></thead><tbody>{''.join(costs)}</tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="images">Inspect common detail and coverage</h2><p>Motion captures freeze the camera at the shared phase 0 outside timed samples. They compare framing and detail at that pose; they do not establish pixel identity through the moving sequence. Lighting and fine edge reconstruction still differ. Open either image at full resolution.</p><label>Terrain <select id="world">{''.join(f'<option value="{k}" {"selected" if k=="nature" else ""}>{v}</option>'for k,v in labels.items())}</select></label> <label>Case <select id="case">{''.join(f'<option value="{k}" {"selected" if k=="dense" else ""}>{v}</option>'for k,v in names.items())}</select></label>{''.join(pictures)}
<h2>Audit and reproduction</h2><p>{data['nativeFrames']:,} native intervals, {data['firefoxFrames']:,} Firefox intervals and {data['gpuSamples']:,} Firefox GPU samples. <a href="{assets}summary.json">Summary</a> · <a href="{assets}method.json">Method and trajectory equations</a> · <a href="{assets}provenance.json">Source provenance</a> · <a href="{assets}validation.json">Validation and exclusions</a> · <a href="../../tools/minecraft-torus/README.md">Reproduction</a></p><ul>{''.join(links)}</ul></main>
<script>function show(){{document.querySelectorAll('.pair').forEach(p=>p.hidden=p.dataset.world!==document.querySelector('#world').value||p.dataset.case!==document.querySelector('#case').value)}}document.querySelectorAll('select').forEach(s=>s.addEventListener('change',show));</script></html>'''
(base/'client/docs/performance-torus-firefox-stress-2026-10-28.html').write_text(html)
